{% extends "layouts/main.j2" %}
{% block main %}
    <section class="static-hero">
        <div class="static-hero-inner">
            {% include "partials/breadcrumbs.j2" %}
            <div class="static-hero-grid">
                <p class="static-hero-label">LLM</p>
                <h1 class="static-hero-title">About our use of Large Language Models</h1>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="static-page">
        <div class="static-page-content">
            <p>
                The Distributed Design Learning Hub relies on both editorial content and curation, and the use of <strong>Large Language Models (LLMs)</strong> to help assist with discovering information and resources in the Distributed Design Platform archive.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Wherever you see text that is<span class="robot-text"><span class="emoji">✨</span> set in a light purple colour, preceded by the "sparkle" emoji</span>, that text has been generated by an LLM</strong>. Such text will always be preceded by a notice linking to this page for further details. Any other content you see on this site has been written, by hand, by a subject-matter expert from the Distributed Design Platform community. At present, LLM-generated text is only used in the responses generated for queries for custom themes, using the search box on the homepage.
            </p>
            <p>
                LLM generated text will always be an automatically generated <strong>summary</strong> of some content from within the Distributed Design Platform archive, and the sources used to generate it will always be linked and prominently referenced.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>If you encounter any LLM generated content which you believe to be inaccurate, discriminatory, or harmful, please <a href="https://airtable.com/appE3FwwATH2Ul3RR/pagMZlVZfvVtZclhU/form"
    target="_blank">report it to us using the feedback form</a>, and we will review it and take action.</strong>
            </p>
            <h2>Some tips for working with LLM generated summaries</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Always corroborate information using the referenced sources</strong>. Our summaries are intended as an aid to discovery, not as a source of factual information in their own right. Use them to guide your research, but please click through to the referenced resource to verify any factual claims.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Bear in mind that <strong>summaries are based on the information in the Distributed Design Platform archive</strong>, and as such, reflect the research, interests, and knowledge of our community in particular. Summaries for broad themes and topics will reflect our community's engagement with them, not give an entire overview of the field.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Always verify factual claims, particularly those liable to change over time</strong>. Our archive contains the archive of outputs of more than 10 years of research, and a lot can change over that time. Please do further research to check that the summarised information is still current!
                </li>
            </ul>
            <h2>Our commitment to responsible use of machine learning technologies</h2>
            <p>
                We are committed to using machine learning technologies such as LLMs in a way which is consistent with the principles of Distributed Design, and to taking concrete steps to mitigate the ecological and social harms to which they can give rise.
            </p>
            <p>In particular, we aim to use LLMs in a way which is:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Transparent</strong>, both in the sense that text generated using an LLM should be explicitly marked as such, and that the processes, algorithms, and data used to generate the text should be explained publicly and understandably. In addition, we aim to be explicit about the limitations of such text in terms of its accuracy or comprehensiveness.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Responsible</strong>, in that we take concrete steps to mitigate the social and environmental risks that LLMs and other machine learning technologies have the potential to present.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Complementary</strong> to our knowledge and skills, and those of the wider Distributed Design community. Our use of LLMs is not intended to replace or supplant domain knowledge or editorial skills, but to augment them.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                We do so through our choice of technologies. Summaries are written by <a href="https://docs.mistral.ai/models" target="_blank">the current Mistral Small model</a>, developed by the Paris-based company Mistral AI. We chose it because:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    It is <strong>open-weight</strong>: the model is published under the permissive Apache 2.0 licence, so anyone can inspect, download, and run it on their own hardware.
                </li>
                <li>
                    It is a <strong>small <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mixture_of_experts"
    target="_blank">mixture-of-experts</a> model</strong>: only a small part of the model is used to produce each word, so it needs much less computing power, and therefore energy and material resources, than the large general-purpose models behind the most popular chatbots. Summarising a few passages that we provide does not need more than that.
                </li>
                <li>
                    It <a href="https://docs.mistral.ai/studio/safety-moderation"
    target="_blank">offers guardrails against discriminatory and harmful content</a>, which we keep switched on. We also leave its optional step-by-step "reasoning" mode switched off, as it would multiply the computation needed for little benefit on such a short task.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Rather than fixing a specific version, we always use Mistral's current Small model, so that we benefit from its improvements without needing to change our code.
            </p>
            <p>
                In addition, this commitment is reflected as a design concern: We use LLMs only as a fallback to generate specific content when we do not have content already available, and we cache responses to ensure that the amount of text generated is kept to a minimum. Each summary is generated once and then reused for everyone who searches for the same theme, and is only regenerated every couple of months, so that it reflects the model we currently use. In addition, our strategy of producing only successive summaries of text from within the Distributed Design platform archive cuts down on the likelihood that discriminatory or harmful content is generated.
            </p>
            <p>
                Finally, we have tested and audited the system for bias and harm pre-launch, and review all generated content on an ongoing basis for accuracy and fairness. We hope that as well as providing a useful service, the Distributed Design Learning Hub might also serve as an example of best-practice for incorporating machine learning technologies in projects in a way that reflects and respects the values of the Distributed Design community.
            </p>
            <h2>The environmental footprint of our summaries</h2>
            <p>
                Every response from an LLM uses electricity, water to cool data centres, and hardware whose manufacture has its own impacts. These are hard to measure precisely, and AI companies publish very different figures, measured in different ways, if they publish any at all. This is what we know as of November 2026:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Mistral AI</strong> has published a <a href="https://mistral.ai/news/our-contribution-to-a-global-environmental-standard-for-ai"
    target="_blank">life-cycle analysis</a> of one of its models, carried out with the consultancy Carbone 4 and the French ecological transition agency ADEME, and reviewed by two independent consultancies. For its much larger Mistral Large 2 model, it estimated <strong>1.14 g of CO₂-equivalent and 45 mL of water</strong> for a 400-token (roughly 300-word) response from its Le Chat assistant, and found that impacts are roughly proportional to model size. The model we use does only a small fraction of Mistral Large 2's computation for each word it writes, and our summaries are only one or two sentences long, so each of them should have a correspondingly smaller footprint.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>OpenAI</strong> has stated that the <a href="https://blog.samaltman.com/the-gentle-singularity"
    target="_blank">average ChatGPT query uses about 0.34 Wh</a> of electricity, without saying which model or what the figure includes, and has not published CO₂ figures per query.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Anthropic</strong> has not, to our knowledge, published energy or CO₂ figures per request for its Claude models.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Google</strong> has published a <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.15734" target="_blank">detailed methodology</a> estimating 0.24 Wh, 0.03 g CO₂e and 0.26 mL of water for a median Gemini text prompt, which is a useful reference point, although it excludes the training of the model.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                The size of the latest GPT and Claude models is not public, so independent estimates have to rely on assumptions. The open-source <a href="https://ecologits.ai/" target="_blank">EcoLogits</a> tool from the non-profit GenAI Impact estimates that a 400-token response from the current Mistral Small model uses about 0.14 Wh, against roughly 1.3 to 3.5 Wh for the largest current models, such as Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-5.5: around 10 to 25 times less energy. The smallest models from other providers, such as Claude Haiku, are estimated to use as little or even less. These are modelled estimates rather than measurements, and other studies reach different numbers, but they agree on the main point: <strong>the size of the model, and whether it "reasons" at length, matters far more than which company makes it</strong>. This is why we use a small model for a small task.
            </p>
            <p>
                Where the computation happens also matters, as the CO₂ emitted to produce electricity varies widely between countries. In 2025, it was around 384 g CO₂e per kWh in the United States, 210 g on average in the EU, and between 35 and 41 g in Sweden and France (<a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/carbon-intensity-electricity"
   target="_blank">Ember, via Our World in Data</a>). The same request can therefore have a carbon footprint several times higher or lower depending on the data centre that answers it. Our requests are processed in Europe (see below), where electricity is on average much less carbon-intensive than in the United States, although the exact mix depends on which data centre answers each request.
            </p>
            <h2>A European model provider</h2>
            <p>
                Mistral AI is headquartered in Paris, which makes it one of the leading developers of language models based in the European Union. Like OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google, it has signed the EU's <a href="https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/contents-code-gpai"
    target="_blank">General-Purpose AI Code of Practice</a> under the AI Act. And because its model is open-weight, it could also be run on independent European infrastructure, should we ever need to.
            </p>
            <p>
                We send all our requests to Mistral's <a href="https://docs.mistral.ai/inference/regional-inference"
    target="_blank">EU regional endpoint</a>, so both the conversion of your search into an embedding and the writing of summaries are processed in data centres in the European Union and EFTA countries. We only send Mistral the theme you searched for and passages from the Distributed Design Platform archive: nothing that identifies you, such as your IP address, is sent. In addition, <a href="https://help.mistral.ai/en/articles/347629" target="_blank">Mistral hosts its customers' data in the European Union</a> by default, although some data may be temporarily transferred outside the EU under GDPR safeguards.
            </p>
            <h2>How we use LLMs to generate summaries</h2>
            <p>
                The Learning Hub search interface operates, broadly, as a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Retrieval-augmented_generation"
    target="_blank">Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)</a> system.
            </p>
            <p>
                Our editorial team have written, by hand, a summary for each document in the Learning Hub archive, and categorised them by hand into thematic areas, and more general tags. This editorial content, plus the text of the document itself, is used to create <strong>embeddings</strong> - mathematical representations of portions of the document which can be compared to each other (and to text queries) in a way that allows us to query the resulting database by semantic similarity.
            </p>
            <p>
                When you search for a custom theme, using the search form on the homepage, your query is also converted into an embedding, from which we retrieve the most similar document fragments to your query (each of them scored by how similar it is). We then collate these by the documents that they were taken from, and sort them so that the most similar document appears first - these are the search results you see alongside the summary on the results page.
            </p>
            <p>
                For these documents we then provide the text of the retrieved fragments to a language model, along with a prompt to summarise how the topic of your query is addressed in the document. Any documents with no relevant information are skipped, and we continue down the list until we have a maximum of three summaries. These are then presented back to you, along with links to the source.
            </p>
            <p>
                Finally, we provide the text of all the fragments of the summarised documents to the language model, along with a prompt to summarise the topic in general, using the information provided. This is added as the first sentence to the summary.
            </p>
            <p>
                For those who want more technical details: our embeddings are created with the <a href="https://docs.mistral.ai/models" target="_blank">mistral-embed</a> model, stored in an <a href="https://www.elastic.co/" target="_blank">elasticsearch</a> database, and similarity is measured by cosine distance between the query and the fragment. We use <a href="https://docs.mistral.ai/models" target="_blank">the current Mistral Small model</a>, through Mistral's EU regional endpoint (which we also use for embeddings), to generate summaries, and <a href="https://docs.llamaindex.ai/en/stable/" target="_blank">llama_index</a> as plumbing for the whole system. The entire application is open source, and <a href="https://github.com/fablabbcn/distributed-design-learning-hub/"
    target="_blank">available on Github</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
{% endblock main %}
